/* data/learn-c/phy-12-16.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-16.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-16"] = {"16.1":{"s":"A p-n junction diode conducts when forward biased and almost blocks current when reverse biased, so it acts like a switch. Mains power is AC, but most electronic gadgets need DC. A power supply therefore uses a transformer, a diode rectifier (AC to pulsating DC), a filter that removes the ripple, and a voltage regulator that keeps the output steady.","w":["A half wave rectifier gives output pulses at the input frequency; a full wave rectifier at twice the input frequency (100 Hz from 50 Hz mains)."],"x":["A p-n junction diode conducts well when it is forward biased, and almost not at all when it is reverse biased. So it works like a one-way switch for current. This is the basic idea behind most of the devices in this chapter.","Mains power is AC, but most electronic devices need steady DC. A power supply does this in four steps. A transformer changes the AC to the voltage that is needed. A rectifier, made with diodes, turns the AC into pulsating DC. A filter, usually a capacitor, smooths the pulses. A voltage regulator, like a Zener diode, keeps the output steady when the input or the load changes.","The chapter then studies the diode as a rectifier, the special diodes, the transistor as a switch and an amplifier, and the logic gates, which are the building blocks of digital circuits.","Common mistake: mixing up the output frequency. A half wave rectifier gives pulses at the input frequency, 50 Hz. A full wave rectifier gives twice that, 100 Hz."],"e":"A mobile charger takes 230 V AC from the wall. Inside, a transformer reduces it to a few volts, diodes rectify it, a capacitor smooths it, and a regulator holds it at 5 V."},"16.2":{"s":"A rectifier changes AC into DC, using the fact that a p-n junction diode conducts in one direction only. A half wave rectifier uses one diode and passes only the positive half cycles. A full wave rectifier uses two diodes on a centre-tapped transformer and uses both half cycles, so it is more efficient. The output still has a small AC part, the ripple, which a filter (a capacitor across the load) reduces.","f":[{"x":"Ripple factor = (r.m.s. value of the AC component)/(value of the DC component)","n":"A smaller ripple factor means a better rectifier.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.2, p.346"},{"x":"f_out = f_in (half wave), f_out = 2*f_in (full wave)","n":"Output frequency: 50 Hz input gives 50 Hz from a half wave and 100 Hz from a full wave rectifier.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.2, p.346"}],"w":["The book gives the largest efficiency as 40.6% for a half wave and 81.2% for a full wave rectifier.","A full wave rectifier needs a centre-tapped transformer and two diodes (or a bridge of four)."],"x":["A rectifier changes AC into DC. A half wave rectifier uses one diode. During the positive half cycle the diode is forward biased and conducts. During the negative half cycle it is reverse biased and blocks. So only half of each cycle reaches the load. Its output frequency is the same as the input frequency, and V_dc = V_m/π.","A full wave rectifier uses both half cycles. The usual circuit has two diodes and a transformer with a centre tap, and each diode conducts in one half cycle. A bridge circuit uses four diodes and needs no centre tap. The output frequency is twice the input frequency, and V_dc = 2V_m/π. The largest efficiency is 40.6 percent for a half wave and 81.2 percent for a full wave rectifier.","The output of a rectifier is not steady. It has an AC part, the ripple. The ripple factor is the rms value of the AC part divided by the DC value. It is 1.21 for a half wave and 0.48 for a full wave rectifier. A smaller value is better. A capacitor across the load charges to the peak and discharges slowly, and this reduces the ripple.","Common mistake: using V_rms in place of the peak value V_m. For a sine wave, V_m = √2 V_rms."],"e":"The AC input has an rms value of 20 V, so V_m = 28.3 V. A half wave rectifier gives V_dc = 28.3/π = 9.0 V. A full wave rectifier gives 2 × 28.3/π = 18.0 V."},"16.3":{"s":"Special purpose diodes. A Zener diode is heavily doped and is used in reverse breakdown, where its voltage stays almost constant, so it works as a voltage regulator. A photodiode is used in reverse bias; light makes its reverse current grow in proportion to the light. A solar cell turns light into electrical power with no bias at all. An LED gives out light when forward biased.","f":[{"x":"P_Z = I_Z(max)*V_Z","n":"Maximum power rating of a Zener diode.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.3, p.349"},{"x":"R_d = (maximum reverse voltage)/(dark current)","n":"Dark resistance of a photodiode (no light falling on it).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.3, p.352"}],"w":["Zener diode and photodiode work in REVERSE bias; an LED in FORWARD bias; a solar cell needs no bias.","In a Zener regulator the series resistor takes up the change in input voltage; the load voltage stays at the Zener voltage.","Printed p.354 calls Voc × Isc the maximum power. It is not: the power is zero at both ends, and the maximum is Vm × Im at the knee of the I-V curve, always below Voc × Isc."],"x":["A Zener diode is heavily doped, and it is made to work in the reverse breakdown region. There, the voltage across it stays almost constant while the current changes a lot. So it is used as a voltage regulator. It is placed in parallel with the load, with a series resistor. If the input voltage rises, the extra current goes through the Zener diode, and the extra voltage drops across the series resistor. The load voltage stays at the Zener voltage. The power rating is P = V_Z × I_Z(max).","A photodiode works in reverse bias. In the dark, only a small dark current flows. When light falls on the junction, it makes electron-hole pairs, and the reverse current increases in proportion to the light. It is used to detect light and in light sensors.","A light emitting diode (LED) is forward biased. The electrons and holes recombine at the junction and give out light. The colour depends on the energy gap of the material. LEDs use little power and last long.","A solar cell needs no external bias. Light makes electron-hole pairs near the junction, and the built-in field separates them to give a voltage. The most power that it gives is at the knee of its I–V curve, and this is less than the product of the open-circuit voltage and the short-circuit current. Common mistake: using forward bias for a Zener diode or a photodiode."],"e":"A Zener diode of 6 V and a 300 Ω series resistor run from 12 V. The current through the resistor is 6/300 = 20 mA. If the load takes 6 mA, the Zener diode takes the other 14 mA, and the load stays at 6 V."},"16.4":{"s":"A bipolar junction transistor has three regions, emitter, base and collector, forming two back-to-back p-n junctions (n-p-n or p-n-p). The emitter is heavily doped, the base is very thin and lightly doped. With the emitter-base junction forward biased and the collector-base junction reverse biased, most carriers from the emitter cross the base to the collector, so a small base current controls a large collector current. In the common emitter configuration this gives current, voltage and power gain.","f":[{"x":"I_E = I_B + I_C","n":"Emitter current is the sum of base and collector currents.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.4, p.359"},{"x":"beta_DC = I_C/I_B","n":"Current gain in common emitter (typically 20-200).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.4, p.359"},{"x":"alpha_DC = I_C/I_E","n":"Current gain in common base (slightly less than 1).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.4, p.359"},{"x":"beta = alpha/(1 - alpha)","n":"Link between the two gains; also alpha = beta/(1 + beta).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.4, p.359"},{"x":"A_v = -beta_AC*R_L/r_i","n":"Voltage gain of a common emitter amplifier; the minus sign means output is 180 degrees out of phase.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.4, p.361"},{"x":"A_p = beta_AC*A_v","n":"Power gain (magnitude).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.4, p.361"}],"w":["A transistor does not create power: the extra output power comes from the DC supply.","The arrow in the symbol is on the emitter and points the way conventional current flows: out for n-p-n, in for p-n-p."],"x":["A bipolar junction transistor (BJT) has three regions: the emitter, the base and the collector. It is of the n-p-n or the p-n-p type. The emitter is heavily doped to give many carriers. The base is very thin and lightly doped, so few carriers are lost in it. The collector is larger, to collect the carriers.","For an amplifier, the emitter-base junction gets a forward bias. The collector-base junction gets a reverse bias. Most of the carriers from the emitter cross the thin base and reach the collector. A small change of the base current causes a large change of the collector current. The currents are related by I_E = I_B + I_C.","The current gain in the common emitter mode is β = I_C/I_B, usually from 20 to 200. In the common base mode, α = I_C/I_E, a little less than 1. They are related by β = α/(1 − α). In a common emitter amplifier, the voltage gain is A_v = −β R_L/r_i. The minus sign means the output is 180° out of phase with the input. The power gain is A_p = β × A_v in magnitude.","A transistor does not create power. The extra output power comes from the DC supply. Common mistake: using β where α is needed, or the reverse."],"e":"A transistor has I_B = 40 μA and I_C = 2.0 mA. β = 2.0 mA/40 μA = 50. I_E = 2.04 mA, so α = 2.0/2.04 = 0.98, and α/(1 − α) = 49 to 50, as it must be."},"16.5":{"s":"A logic gate is a digital circuit with one or more inputs and one output, each either high (1) or low (0). The NOT gate inverts its input. The OR gate gives 1 if any input is 1; the AND gate gives 1 only if all inputs are 1. NAND and NOR are the opposites of AND and OR; they are universal gates, since any gate can be built from them. The XOR gate gives 1 when its inputs differ.","f":[{"x":"Y = NOT X","n":"NOT gate (inverter): the output is the opposite of the input.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.5, p.362"},{"x":"Y = A + B","n":"OR gate (logical addition): 1 + 1 = 1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.5, p.362"},{"x":"Y = A·B","n":"AND gate (logical multiplication): 1 only if both inputs are 1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.5, p.362"},{"x":"Y = NOT(A·B)","n":"NAND gate: AND followed by NOT.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.5, p.362"},{"x":"Y = NOT(A + B)","n":"NOR gate: OR followed by NOT.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.5, p.362"},{"x":"C = A ⊕ B","n":"XOR gate: 1 when exactly one input is 1.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 16.5, p.363"}],"w":["In Boolean algebra + means OR and a dot means AND; 1 + 1 = 1.","NAND and NOR are universal gates; AND, OR and XOR are not."],"x":["A logic gate is a digital circuit that has one or more inputs and one output. Each is either high (1) or low (0). The behaviour of a gate is shown in a truth table, which lists the output for every combination of the inputs.","The NOT gate inverts: Y = A′. The OR gate gives 1 if any input is 1: Y = A + B. The AND gate gives 1 only if all the inputs are 1: Y = A·B. The NAND gate is an AND followed by NOT, and the NOR gate is an OR followed by NOT. The XOR gate gives 1 when the inputs are different: Y = A ⊕ B.","In Boolean algebra, + means OR, and a dot means AND, so 1 + 1 = 1. NAND and NOR are called universal gates, because any other gate can be built from them. For example, a NAND gate with its inputs joined is a NOT gate. AND, OR and XOR are not universal.","To find the output of a circuit, work from the inputs, gate by gate, and write down the result of each step. Common mistake: forgetting the NOT at the end of NAND and NOR gates."],"e":"Inputs A = 1 and B = 0. AND gives 0. OR gives 1. NAND gives 1. NOR gives 0. XOR gives 1."}};
